<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Epaminondas</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">c. 418–<a href="/years/362-bce">362 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century-bce">4th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Epaminondas was a Theban general and statesman who, with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a>, made <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> the leading power of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. His deep, left-weighted <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phalanx">phalanx</a> defeated <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sparta">Sparta</a> at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a> in 371 BCE. He then invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a>, freed Messenia from Spartan rule and backed the founding of Messene and Megalopolis. He was killed at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-mantinea-362-bce">Battle of Mantinea</a> in 362 BCE, and Theban supremacy faded soon after.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/epaminondas.jpg" alt="Epaminondas" width="320" height="516" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Daderot · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Epaminondas_-_Temple_of_Ancient_Virtue,_Stowe_-_Buckinghamshire,_England_-_DSC07376_(cropped).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Epaminondas was born at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> to a family of high standing which, by tradition, claimed descent from the mythical Spartoi. The Pythagorean Lysis of Tarentum, who had fled persecution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magna-graecia">Magna Graecia</a> and lived in his father's house, taught him philosophy and influenced him deeply. Ancient writers describe him as patriotic, incorruptible and modest, living in voluntary poverty. He never married, and his lifelong companion was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a>.</p><p>After its victory in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnesian-war">Peloponnesian War</a> in 404 BCE Sparta followed an aggressively unilateral policy toward the rest of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>. In 382 BCE the Spartan commander <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/phoebidas">Phoebidas</a>, taking advantage of civil strife in Thebes, seized the Theban acropolis, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmeia</a>, and the anti-Spartan party was forced to flee. Epaminondas was allowed to remain, because his philosophy and poverty made him seem harmless.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>The exiles regrouped in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> and prepared to liberate the city, while Epaminondas prepared the young men of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> to fight. A small group of exiles led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pelopidas">Pelopidas</a> slipped into the city and killed the leaders of the pro-Spartan government, and with Epaminondas and Athenian hoplites they surrounded the Spartans on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cadmea">Cadmeia</a>, who surrendered on condition of marching away unharmed. In 371 BCE Epaminondas won the battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/battle-of-leuctra">Leuctra</a>, where his decision to refuse his right flank, the first recorded use of such a tactic, let a smaller force defeat the Spartan phalanx.</p><p>The next year the Thebans invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peloponnese">Peloponnese</a> to break Spartan power. The historian Hans Beck argues that <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> made no effort to create an empire or bind its allies in a stable organisation. The poem on his tomb, as Pausanias records it, credits him with the restoration of Messene and the walls of Megalopolis.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>At Mantinea in 362 BCE Epaminondas was hit in the chest by a spear while pressing forward with his troops. Carried to camp still alive, he asked which side had won, and on hearing that the Boeotians had, said that it was time to die. He was buried on the battlefield. Within a few years <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/athens">Athens</a> replaced <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thebes-greece">Thebes</a> at the head of Greek politics. Thebes was defeated by Philip II at Chaeronea in 338 BCE, and three years later <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-the-great">Alexander the Great</a> destroyed the city after a revolt. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cicero">Cicero</a> called Epaminondas the first man of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Epaminondas” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Under Theban leadership in the fourth century Pythagorean teaching reached the elite through Lysis of Tarentum, who taught <b>Epaminondas</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/boeotian-league">Boeotian League · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>At the Battle of Leuctra in 371 BCE the Thebans under <b>Epaminondas</b> defeated the Spartan army and killed King Cleombrotus I, and in 370–369 BCE they invaded Laconia, freed Messenia and founded the city of Messene, taking away half of Sparta's helot land.</blockquote><a href="/empire/sparta">Sparta · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Trained and led by <b>Epaminondas</b> and Pelopidas, with the Sacred Band of Thebes, the Theban army won at Leuctra in 371 BC, then carried its arms into the Peloponnesus, freeing many helots, and sent expeditions to Thessaly and Macedon.</blockquote><a href="/empire/thebes">Thebes · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Thebans under <b>Epaminondas</b>, with their allies, defeated the Spartans, Eleans, Athenians and Mantineans in a battle to decide which alliance would dominate Greece.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-4">4 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The Thebans and their Boeotian allies under <b>Epaminondas</b> defeated a Spartan army near Leuctra in Boeotia, killing King Cleombrotus I. The defeat ended the belief in Spartan invincibility and began a decade of Theban supremacy in Greece.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/july-6">6 July in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Refounded in 370 BCE, Mantinea helped create the Arcadian League, and in 362 BCE <b>Epaminondas</b> died winning the second Battle of Mantinea.</blockquote><a href="/state/mantinea">Mantinea</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Thebes, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. Died in Pelagos forest, in what is now <a href="/countries/greece">Greece</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=38.32,23.32&amp;year=-382" aria-label="Thebes on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="5130 787 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Epaminondas"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5580 1057h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5580 1057h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=38.32,23.32&amp;year=-382">What was here in 382 BCE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
